import ArchonPhysics.CanonicalIIDCoerciveHaarMatchedCouplingPower
import ArchonPhysics.FreeFPUTA0IteratedQuadraticFeedbackIdentity
import ArchonPhysics.FreeFPUTBranchingHistoryDenominatorEnumeration
import ArchonPhysics.FreeFPUTDirectCubicA0InterferenceCancellation

/-!
# Structural rank of Haar-matched FPUT denominator forests

`orderedHistoryDenominators` records exponentially many denominator
*occurrences*.  Their additive rank is much smaller: every occurrence is an
integer combination of the local vertex phases.  This file proves that
statement for ordered and branching histories and then inserts the one
relation that really follows from a Haar-matched pair with a common root:
the sums of its two local-phase lists agree.

The resulting universal forest generator budget for two nontrivial trees is

`left.order + right.order - 1`.

It is not, in general, the arithmetic interaction-pair count
`(left.order + right.order) / 2`.  A balanced pair of two order-two binary
trees below has three linearly independent local phase forms, whereas its
derived interaction-pair count is two.  Thus charge matching alone cannot
prove the stronger rank bound used by a square-cutoff argument.

At physical order two the exact result is sharper by channel.  The retained
`g^2` A1/A1 and A0/A2 histories have respectively one, one, and zero
generators.  In the `g^4` A2/A2 square, direct/direct has one generator and
iterated/direct has two, but the generic iterated/iterated sector has the
sharp three-generator obstruction.  Consequently the mixed and direct
`g^4` regular-good pieces have the existing quadratic kinetic cutoff, while
the absolute-value iterated/iterated piece is exactly on the infeasible
boundary.  No RPA, spectral transversality, or cancellation of that boundary
sector is asserted.
-/

